[candidate · 12:07] I would pull the COGS split by line item, for both year, per unit. So:

One, green coffee bean — cost per pound, and how many pound per cup, because waste can change too.

Two, dairy — milk, cream, and alternative milk like oat, almond. Those alternative are much more expensive, and if customer shift to them and the store do not charge enough surcharge, that raise COGS with no price change.

Three, packaging — cup, lid, sleeve, straw.

Four, food and pastry, if they sell food. Food margin is usually worse than drink, so if food become bigger part of mix, blended COGS go up.

Five, waste and spoilage rate, and any theft or free-drink policy.

Then I would decompose: how much of the sixteen point is pure price of input, how much is mix shift, how much is waste. Because the fix is different — for input price you negotiate or hedge or raise menu price, for mix you reprice the oat milk, for waste you fix operation.

