[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 15:54] Beyond pricing, what else could the client do to recover margin? Give me a structured set.

[candidate · 17:24] Okay. Let me structure in four lever, outside of price.

First, procurement. Long-term contract or hedging on bean, so they are not exposed to spot. Consolidate volume across 200 store for better term. Second source for dairy and packaging. Maybe re-spec the cup, cheaper lid.

Second, product cost engineering. Portion control — grams of bean per shot, how much milk in each size. Reduce waste and spoilage, especially pastry that expire. Also reformulate: push the drink that use less of the expensive input.

Third, mix. Actively steer customer to high-margin item — drip coffee, or the drink with less dairy. Bundle, loyalty reward on those. And if oat milk is the problem, add a surcharge, which is technically price but it is mix too.

Fourth, the non-COGS line. Overhead went twelve to fourteen, that is two point, worth attacking — corporate cost, marketing efficiency. Labor is flat at twenty-two so it is not broken, but scheduling optimization could still give some.

Realistically though, these give maybe five, six point. Price must carry the rest.
